 | `present_interval` | `game` (the default): frames are paced by the present interval the game sets, as on the Switch (a frame presented with interval 2 stays on screen for two display ticks; a game that keeps asking for 2 for a second gets the 30 fps target, and on iOS the display link then runs at 30 Hz; Mario 1.2.1 switches to 2 for single frames after a slow one; Engage asks for 2 on the Switch, and a rewriter cut on its 2.0.0 `main` makes it ask for 1, see `0100A6301214E000/game.tsv`). `display`: one frame per display tick whatever the game asks, which is only right for games whose logic does not count frames (D2R's runs on its own 25 Hz clock; Monster Train 2 asks for 2 and steps by Unity's frame time, so it runs at 60 fps: screenshots at the same moments match at 30 and 60 fps). `DM_PRESENT_INTERVAL=game` / `display` overrides it, also from `Documents/env.txt`; with `display` and no `fps_target`, the launcher's Frame rate sets it (30 fps = `game`) |
 | `present_snap` | `on` / `off` (the default): present snapshots by default (`nvn_mtl.m` `mtl_snap_on`, a ring of 2 host textures): each present is copied and the game's swapchain texture is free at once, so the GPU does not idle between frames ([Present snapshots](../../docs/reference/frame-rate.md#present-snapshots-the-games-swapchain-texture-free-at-once)). A setting the game was tested with: the iOS launcher's **Frame buffering** defaults to it, and `DM_PRESENT_SNAP` / `Documents/env.txt` override it. Breath of the Wild sets it on |
 | `pso_mtl4` | `on` / `off` (the default): on iOS, render pipelines through MTL4Compiler by default, with the pipeline archive, the prepare step, the hybrid pipelines and the prebuild (on by default on the Mac; `DM_PSO_MTL4=1` / `0` overrides). A setting the game was tested with on an iPhone. It also gives the app the [pipeline archive made offline](../../docs/development.md#the-pipeline-archive-made-offline) (`build_ipa.sh` builds it for the `pso_gpu` key's list when present, else for `g18p`, unless `DM_PSO_GPUS` says otherwise). An iOS build carries the pulling libraries (`_bp`) whatever this key says, since the launcher's Metal 4 pipelines toggle can turn the path on. Profiles with `pso_mtl4 off` get no archive unless a build override selects one. Breath of the Wild sets it on |
+| `link_latency30` | `1` (the default) / `2`: the display link's frame latency at a 30 fps cap (`nvn_mtl.m` `link_latency`; `DM_PRESENT_LATENCY` overrides). 2 gives a GPU-bound frame two 30 Hz ticks (66 ms) from submission to display, for one frame more input lag at 30 fps only. Ocarina of Time: 2, as an iPhone lowers the GPU clock at 30 fps until the work fills the frame |
 | `pso_gpu` | Optional, whitespace-separated GPU architectures: the ones the [pipeline archive made offline](../../docs/development.md#the-pipeline-archive-made-offline) is made for when the profile also sets `pso_mtl4 on`. The bases are made per GPU family and each family's archive costs its own `metal-tt` run, so a profile names exactly the devices it serves. 1-4 tokens, each alphanumeric, with or without the `applegpu_` prefix (for example `g18p applegpu_g19p`); `none` is not a value. Without this key, `pso_mtl4 on` keeps the A19 / A19 Pro default `g18p`; `pso_mtl4 off` gets no archive. The `DM_PSO_GPUS` switch / the NSP script's `--gpu` overrides the archive selection; this key does not enable Metal 4 pipelines |
 | `nvn_prefetch` | `on` (the default) / `off`: a program's shader libraries loaded in the background when the game makes it (`nvn_mtl.m` `mtl_prog_prefetch`), so a scene's first draws do not stall. `DM_NVN_PREFETCH` and the launcher's Shader prefetch override it. On iOS a program's libraries are then mapped whether a draw uses them or not: a game with a very large shader tree fills the process's mapping limit and the system ends it (Persona 5 Royal, 24,826 pipelines, about ten minutes). Persona 5 Royal sets it off |
 | `enc_thread` | `on` / `off` (the default): on the Mac, Metal encoding on its own thread by default, as on iOS for every game (`nvn_rec.h` `rec_on`; `DM_NVN_ENC_THREAD=1` / `0` and the launcher's Encode thread override it). For a game that waits on its fence every frame, whose submit thread would otherwise also encode before the wait: Need for Speed: Hot Pursuit Remastered's 60 fps frame took 18.1 ms with encoding on the submit thread and 16.7 on its own |
 | `fps_target` | Frame rate cap of the rewriter's frame step, for builds that have one (`FRAME_STEP` in the profile's `patches.py`: Tears of the Kingdom 1.4.3, Breath of the Wild 1.9.0, Hyrule Warriors: Age of Calamity 1.3.0, Paper Mario: The Thousand-Year Door 1.0.1, ASTRAL CHAIN 1.0.1), or the engine's own frame limit the host writes into game memory (`hle_fpslock.c`: Monster Hunter Rise 16.0.2, which also needs `present_interval display`; any Unreal Engine 4.2x / 5 build, its `t.MaxFPS` (or `ue_fps_cvar`) and `rhi.SyncInterval` or `nvn.SyncInterval`, found by name by the rewriter, so the game asks for present interval 1 itself, and its fixed frame rate `GEngine->FixedFrameRate`, found by its reflection entry and written while the engine's `bUseFixedFrameRate` is set (DRAGON BALL: Sparking! ZERO's battles, Pikmin 4); Luigi's Mansion 3 1.4.0, its frame limiter's milliseconds per frame, with `present_interval display`; Pokémon Brilliant Diamond 1.3.0, Unity's integer `Application.targetFrameRate`, also with `present_interval display`; EA SPORTS FC 27, Frostbite's simulation rate, found by name on any build, and on 1.8d.310c its frame regulator, also with `present_interval display`; Fire Emblem Fortune's Weave, a target frame rate the game steps by, found by its readers on any build, so the game asks for present interval 1 itself). `0` (the default): the game's own step. `30` / `60`: the host decides each frame's step in whole vsyncs and keeps the frame on screen that long (`hle_nvn.c` `fstep_tick`, `nvn_fstep.h`): 1 at a 60 cap, 2 when 60 does not hold or at a 30 cap (and while a prerendered movie plays), plus one vsync while the game is behind real time after a late frame. Presents follow that hold instead of the interval the game sets. `DM_FPS_TARGET` overrides it, also from `Documents/env.txt`; the log line `frame step: game time … of real time` checks the game's speed. A derived profile (`derive_profile`) whose `main` has no `FRAME_STEP` stubs, while the profile has them for other builds, gets `fps_target 0`: without the stubs the host would present every vsync and the game would run at double speed. On a main with neither a frame step nor an engine limit the host writes, the host keeps the game's own present interval whatever `fps_target` or `DM_FPS_TARGET` says (`hle_fpslock.c` `dm_fps_lock_engine`), except with `present_interval display`, where the cap is the present interval alone: a game whose only limit is its present interval and that steps by the time that passed (Red Dead Redemption) |
 | `fps_max` | `60` (the default) or `120`: the highest rate `fps_target` and the player's **Frame rate** take. `120` only for a game with an engine limit the host writes and whose speed was measured at 120 against 60 over the same inputs: the target then follows the frames that fit, 120 or 60 ([120 fps follows the frame step](../../docs/reference/frame-rate.md#120-fps-follows-the-frame-step)). |
 | `fps_hold` | `on` (the default): with `fps_target`, three late frames in a row raise the hold above the cap's vsyncs (30 or 20 fps) when the last 30 frames also fall short of the cap on average, and steady frames that fit try the lower hold again. `off`: frames are never held longer than the cap's vsyncs; each is shown as soon as it is ready. For a game whose updates do not wait for the presents and step by the cap whatever the hold (Age of Calamity): there the hold only paces the screen. `DM_FPS_HOLD=on` / `off` overrides it, also from `Documents/env.txt` Paper Mario: The Thousand-Year Door sets it off too: its frame waits for three render worker threads, and holding frames left them idle, so a frame cost more the longer the hold (the hold stuck at 3 vsyncs, 20 fps, on a loaded Mac; 32–58 fps without holds) |
+| `fps_hold` | `on` (the default): with `fps_target`, three late frames in a row raise the hold above the cap's vsyncs (30 or 20 fps), and steady frames that fit try the lower hold again. `off`: frames are never held longer than the cap's vsyncs; each is shown as soon as it is ready. For a game whose updates do not wait for the presents and step by the cap whatever the hold (Age of Calamity): there the hold only paces the screen. `DM_FPS_HOLD=on` / `off` overrides it, also from `Documents/env.txt` Paper Mario: The Thousand-Year Door sets it off too: its frame waits for three render worker threads, and holding frames left them idle, so a frame cost more the longer the hold (the hold stuck at 3 vsyncs, 20 fps, on a loaded Mac; 32–58 fps without holds) |
+| `fps_payback` | `on` (the default): with `fps_target`, real time a late frame lost is paid back with a step one vsync longer than the hold (game time = real time). `off`: the step stays the hold and the time is let go, so the game slows by a late frame instead of a half-frame jump in motion on the next one (`hle_nvn.c` `fstep_payback`, `nvn_fstep.h` `nopay`; `DM_FPS_PAYBACK=on` / `off` overrides). Ocarina of Time: off |
 | `ue_fps_cvar` | Unreal Engine games: the console variable the host writes `fps_target` into (`hle_fpslock.c`). `t.MaxFPS` (the default) is the engine's frame limit; a game whose settings apply a variable of its own to `t.MaxFPS` names that one, or the next settings change sets the game's 30 again (SHIN MEGAMI TENSEI V: Vengeance: `Project.FrameRateLimit`). The rewriter finds it by its registered name, so a name set here must be in `dm/tools/ue_cvar.py`'s `NAMES` (a red line otherwise): [Unreal's frame limit and present interval, found by name](../../docs/reference/frame-rate.md#unreals-frame-limit-and-present-interval-found-by-name) |
 | `ue_texture_pool` | Unreal Engine games: megabytes of the texture streaming pool (`r.Streaming.PoolSize`) the host raises the game's own to, on the Mac (`hle_uecvar.c`; the rewriter finds the variable by its name, `dm/tools/ue_cvar.py`): past its pool the streamer drops the top mips of what is in view, and the Switch's pool is small (DRAGON QUEST VII Reimagined: 200 MB, 1000 on Windows). `0` (the default): the game's own pool. A larger value the game sets, or −1, stays. `DM_UE_TEXTURE_POOL` overrides it; on iOS only that switch turns it on (`Documents/env.txt`). An improvement over the Switch, not a fix: [Unreal's texture streaming pool](../../docs/reference/rendering.md#unreals-texture-streaming-pool) |
 | `core_preempt` | `on` / `off` (the default): additionally check higher-priority runnable threads pinned to the caller's core after every thread SVC (`hle_thread.c`). `DM_PREEMPT=0` / `1` overrides it. Wake-triggered same-core handoffs apply to every game with either setting and wait until the threads the wake made runnable block, with no wall-clock cap. Metroid Dread no longer needs this key or its movie request-ring gates; see [Same-core wake handoffs](../../docs/reference/threads.md#same-core-wake-handoffs) |
@@ -253,6 +256,9 @@ One row per profile; the notes are each profile's `README.md`.
 | `pad_layout` | The iPhone virtual gamepad's layout on first launch: `simple` (the default) = 2D (left stick, A/B/X/Y, shoulders, − / +), `full` = 3D (adds a D-pad, a right stick and L3 / R3; the right stick is not drawn: a touch landing in the right third of the screen on no control holds it, centred where it lands), `touch` = Tap (no control but the layout button: every touch goes to the game's touch screen; needs `op_mode handheld`, Balatro). The layout button at the top centre goes to the next layout in game (Tap only in handheld mode) and the app remembers the player's choice (`DmGamepad.m`) |
 | `tex_flush_upload` | `on` / `off` (the default): a texture the game fills from the CPU in place (no packaged layout, no `WriteTexels`) and publishes with `nvnMemoryPoolFlushMappedRange` is uploaded from guest memory like a packaged one, and re-uploaded when its pages change (`ft_add` in `hle_nvn.c`). Tears of the Kingdom streams all its textures this way; without it the 3D scene is black. Off by default because a flushed range in another game could cover an image a shader writes, whose GPU content the upload would replace. Without the key, every game still gets a texture's storage once when the game flushed it before the texture's first use (`nvn_flushed` in `hle_nvn.c`: Breath of the Wild's sky tables); the key is for textures the CPU writes again after that |
 | `tex_dirty_upload` | `on` / `off` (the default), needs `tex_flush_upload`: a registered texture whose pages the CPU writes after `nvnTextureInitialize` without any NVN flush is uploaded as well (its pages are write-protected at registration, checked on first use; `nvn_ft_written` in `hle_nvn.c`). Tears of the Kingdom fills its ASTC terrain material arrays this way; without it the cave is magenta. FINAL FANTASY TACTICS - The Ivalice Chronicles loads its UI textures straight into CPU-uncached pools, which need no flush, after creating them; without both keys its screen stays black. The Great Ace Attorney Chronicles copies every texture into a CPU-cached pool after creating it and never flushes (its `main` has no cache maintenance at all): black too. The Legend of Zelda: Ocarina of Time writes its terrain tiles' height, blend and hole maps this way, and without the keys has holes in the ground |
+| `render_cond` | `on` / `off` (the default): `SetRenderEnableConditional` / `SetRenderEnable` are recorded and evaluated at replay, and a timestamp report's counter field is 0 as on the GPU (`OP_RENDER_COND` in `nvn_mtl.m`). Tears of the Kingdom wraps its clears in conditions that skip clearing a target nothing has drawn into since its last clear; without the key every clear runs and its 3D scene is black. Animal Crossing: New Horizons too: four clear-only passes wiped its composite before the window copy, and every frame was black. Off by default: other games draw everything, as before |
+| `pool_skip_retired` | `on` / `off` (the default): a pool table for handles traced to no slot (`pool_gpu` in `nvn_mtl.m`) gives a texture ID whose texture the game has finalized, and that holds no view, the dummy texture instead of a Metal texture made again from its storage (`DM_NVN_POOL_SKIP_RETIRED=1` / `0` overrides it). Ocarina of Time leaves the old scene's IDs registered at a scene change, and each table rebuild made their 1,500 textures (2.5 GB) again until GPU memory ran out. Off by default: on the console a handle to a finalized texture still reads its storage while that is there, so a game that keeps sampling through such handles would read the dummy ([Finalized textures made again by pool tables](../../docs/reference/nvn-objects.md#finalized-textures-made-again-by-pool-tables)) |
+| `tex_dirty_upload` | `on` / `off` (the default), needs `tex_flush_upload`: a registered texture whose pages the CPU writes after `nvnTextureInitialize` without any NVN flush is uploaded as well (its pages are write-protected at registration, checked on first use; `nvn_ft_written` in `hle_nvn.c`). Tears of the Kingdom fills its ASTC terrain material arrays this way; without it the cave is magenta. FINAL FANTASY TACTICS - The Ivalice Chronicles loads its UI textures straight into CPU-uncached pools, which need no flush, after creating them; without both keys its screen stays black. The Great Ace Attorney Chronicles copies every texture into a CPU-cached pool after creating it and never flushes (its `main` has no cache maintenance at all): black too |
 | `tex_dyn_slots` | `on` / `off` (the default): a shader whose buffer-texture handle the translator could not trace to one slot gets Ryujinx's fallback arrays (`<stage>_<tb|s>_cb0_40000000`: 32 buffer textures and samplers indexed by the handle's texture / sampler ID; any other sampled texture indexes the whole pool without a key, [Texture handles from game memory index the pools](../../docs/reference/shaders.md#texture-handles-from-game-memory-index-the-pools)). With the key the replay puts slot indices in that shader's c2 texture and sampler tables (texture slot r → r, sampler slot r → r << 20) and binds slot k at element k, so the shader's own arithmetic picks the binding (`NVN_RB_TEXD` / `NVN_RB_SAMPD` in `nvn_mtl.m`). Right only where the handle comes from c2's tables: Octopath Traveler 0's 61 such vertex shaders read separate texture slots 0-10 and samplers 0-4 there and nothing else (FINAL FANTASY RESONANCE's 2,077 the texture table words 0x168-0x1c0 and sampler table words 0x568-0x590). Off, such shaders do not load. Not part of the shader chain version |
 | `draw_barriers` | `full` (the default) / `game`: how draw passes are ordered. `full`: each waits for all earlier GPU work. `game`: by the game's own barriers (NVN asks for one before results of rendering are read), so a pass's vertex work overlaps the previous pass's fragments. Ocarina of Time's GPU time 25 → 18 ms, about 180 full barriers a frame halved. Only a barrier that orders primitives or indirect data waits for all earlier work; a fragment-only one keeps the overlap. Only vertex-stage reads of the previous pass's output without a barrier change order. `DM_NVN_BARRIER` overrides it |
 | `vconv_range` | `on` / `off` (the default): vertex attributes the replay converts on the CPU (integer shader inputs fed float-type formats and the like, `vconv_bind` in `nvn_mtl.m`) are converted only over the vertices each draw reads (first / count, or the smallest and largest index of a direct indexed draw, kept while the index pages stay unwritten) instead of the whole bound range. Age of Calamity binds whole mesh pools: in a battle that was millions of vertices a frame. Indirect draws, per-instance streams and indices with a GPU write queued on them keep the whole range. `DM_NVN_VCONV_CHECK=1` scans the indices again at every reuse and reports differences; `DM_NVN_VCONV_RANGE=0` / `1` overrides it |
